I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, and frozen reference/index tooling, then build and checkpoint a complete multiphase GSAS-II model before testing any optional alternatives. I’ll keep the phase search tied to chemistry, peak families, and whole-inventory balance.

The chemistry points first to Mn–W–O products plus residual WO₃/Mn oxide, but I’m treating that only as a prior. Next I’m extracting the observed peak families and querying the supplied frozen index by exact composition/topology so the initial model is evidence-driven.

The peak positions match wolframite-type MnWO₄ essentially line-for-line, including the diagnostic 15.5°, 18.5°, 23.7/24.2°, 30.0/30.4° families and the dense high-angle pattern. I’m now moving into GSAS-II refinement; residual-family analysis after a physically bounded one-phase fit will decide whether any secondary phase is actually required.

The one-phase fit is already enough to expose the main refinement issue: MnWO₄ is correct, but unconstrained profile parameters became nonphysical (negative U/X) because specimen and instrumental broadening are strongly correlated. I’m preserving the last physical checkpoint and will re-stage width terms within the laboratory bounds instead of accepting that numerical minimum.

The residual is not random: a second coherent family is obvious (about 19.86, 20.40, 23.08, 28.62, 33.23/33.52/34.12/35.11°, continuing through high angle). I’m testing the exact precursor polymorphs first—WO₃ and Mn₃O₄—and will require the final inventory to respect the starting 1:1 Mn:W balance.

The topology screen is decisive: monoclinic γ-WO₃ (P2₁/n) gives the only large, stable improvement (Rwp 29.20→22.57%, ~17.5 wt% at this early stage). Triclinic and orthorhombic WO₃ fit materially worse, tetragonal is weak, and hexagonal collapses to ~0.1 wt%. The retained P2₁/n model is also the ambient physical polymorph. I’ve saved this two-phase checkpoint and am now mapping what remains.

The accepted MnWO₄+γ-WO₃ model leaves a broad third family that neither ambient Mn₃O₄ nor Mn₂O₃ explains; both scales collapse when tested stably. MnO accounts for only a small part (~2 wt%) and does not close the 1:1 metal balance. The residual spacings and balance instead motivate Mn-rich tungstates, so I’m now comparing a small topology-complete set of Mn₂WO₆ analogue models (transition-metal parents first), with explicit substitution provenance and geometry checks.

The analogue ranking is still computing full Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ profiles over bounded cell scales and widths. This is only a focused seven-model comparison; no broad database sweep is being used.

The early comparable branches favor the orthorhombic P2₁2₁2₁ Mn₂WO₆ model once heavy-atom coordinates are allowed (Rwp ≈20.18% before profile relaxation, ~11 wt%); the chemically closer trirutile branch is much weaker. I’m letting all branches finish, then I’ll screen their refined bond geometry before selecting—profile gain alone will not decide it.

The decisive issue is now topology rather than peak existence: the residual family is real, but unconstrained coordinate refinements produced impossible bond geometries. I’m repairing the remaining Mn₃WO₆ setting so it can be compared fairly against the physically sound Mn₂WO₆ models, then I’ll carry only the geometry-valid branch into the final refinement.

The repaired Mn₃WO₆ model now imports correctly. Its cell-only fit is already slightly better than every Mn₂WO₆ topology tested on the same three-phase state (Rwp 21.70% versus 21.89–22.38%), and its ~7 wt% scale is stable. I’m letting the identical staged test finish, but any apparent gain that breaks Mn–O/W–O geometry will be rolled back.

The geometry screen rejects the free-coordinate Mn₃WO₆ result (sub-ångström Mn–O contacts), just as it rejected the nominally lower-Rwp Mn₂WO₆ refinements. The unrefined Mn₃WO₆ parent itself is chemically sound—Mn–O ≈2.06–2.46 Å and W–O ≈1.74–1.75 Å—so the next comparison will hold coordinates fixed and give every viable topology the same cell and profile freedom.

The coordinate-fixed comparison is running more slowly because the broadening parameters are strongly coupled, but it is proceeding on the saved physical checkpoint. The acceptance rule remains strict: stable nonzero scale, indexed residual family, chemically sensible cell/geometry, and improvement under the same profile model.

The first fixed-coordinate result confirms a stable three-phase solution, but it is not yet compelling: Mn₃WO₆ remains ~11 wt% and improves Rwp to 16.39%, while its size and some width terms sit at allowed bounds. I’m now applying the identical treatment to the best physical Mn₂WO₆ topology; this comparison will distinguish a genuine topology match from broad-peak absorption.

Peak-by-peak mapping resolves the ambiguity: the third family matches the P2₁2₁2₁ Mn₂WO₆ analogue at 19.90, 20.37, 28.63, 34.82, 35.61, 45.90, 53.85, and 56.67°; competing Mn₃WO₆ and trirutile models lack several of those reflections. I’m therefore stopping phase/prototype search and returning to this physical checkpoint for bounded structural and specimen refinement.

The remaining mismatch is mainly relative intensity within the accepted Mn₂WO₆ family—especially an overpredicted reflection near 21.2°—not an unindexed peak set. I’m testing a restrained relaxation that keeps W–O near 1.87 Å and Mn–O near 2.23 Å, so it can change structure factors without repeating the nonphysical unconstrained solution.

A restrained heavy-atom adjustment gives a substantial fit gain without the catastrophic contacts, but one W–O distance is still marginally short (~1.64 Å). I’m tightening the allowed displacement once more; the oxygen relaxation is being rejected because it worsens Rwp and drives W–O to ~1.52 Å.

The tightened heavy-atom model passes geometry (Mn–O ≈2.10–2.42 Å; W–O ≈1.68–2.01 Å) and improves Rwp from 15.30% to 12.93%. I’ve frozen that accepted structure and am now re-optimizing cells, widths, displacement, surface roughness, polarization, and axial divergence in separate stages so each added family can be audited.

The refinement is still in the coupled profile stage; this is computationally heavy because phase-specific size and strain sit near limits. No additional phase search is occurring, and the last complete replayable physical checkpoint remains preserved if this branch fails or overreaches.

The coupled profile branch was rejected: it raised Rwp to 14.41% and developed singular width parameters. Surface-roughness refinement from the frozen checkpoint also worsened the profile, so those additions are unsupported. I’m independently replaying the accepted project now, because GSAS-II’s stored Rwp after restrained-coordinate work must be verified against a fresh structure-factor calculation before it can be trusted.

The independent replay caught a GSAS-II cache inconsistency: the apparent 12.93% restrained-coordinate fit regenerates at 15.33%, so that coordinate change is rejected despite acceptable geometry. This is exactly why the final project is being checked by fresh reflection generation. I’m reverting to the unmodified physical Mn₂WO₆ coordinates and will only retain stages whose replayed Rwp—not cached Rwp—improves.

Fresh replay establishes 14.82% as the current verified fit (no replay flags), with fractions near 63.0% MnWO₄, 20.7% WO₃, and 16.3% Mn₂WO₆. I’m screening bounded March–Dollase axes one phase at a time; early MnWO₄ ratios stay near 1.0, while WO₃ shows the first meaningful texture response.

The accepted Mn₂WO₆ [001] texture is reproducible on fresh replay (MD ≈1.247; Rwp 14.48%). A second texture family is showing only modest incremental changes; I’m completing that bounded check and will keep it only if its independently replayed improvement survives and its ratio is clearly nontrivial.

Bounded coordinate tests on the reference-backed phases survive fresh replay: refining MnWO₄ heavy atoms gives the best verified result so far, Rwp 14.13%, while WO₃ heavy atoms give 14.19%. The oxygen-stage gains were cache artifacts and are rejected. I’m checking bond geometry, then I’ll combine only the two independently supported heavy-atom adjustments.

A key model-convention mismatch is now evident: the synthetic reference library normalizes every atom to a common isotropic Uiso = 0.005 Å² before specimen-specific Uiso is applied, whereas the imported COD files carried mixed anisotropic tensors (MnWO₄), zeros (WO₃), and 0.01 Å² (analogue). I’m correcting this explicitly and refining only one constrained Uiso per phase within the stated 0.003–0.020 Å² range.

The constrained-ADP solve itself proved numerically unsuitable because dependent atoms escaped their limits, so it is rejected. A fixed common-Uiso grid is stable and physically transparent: MnWO₄ clearly favors the lower allowed end (~0.003 Å²), while the remaining phase grids are still running.

The Uiso correction is validated on fresh replay: Rwp is now 13.75%, with 61.18% MnWO₄, 20.97% WO₃, and 17.85% Mn₂WO₆. The selected common Uiso values are 0.003, 0.003, and 0.020 Å², all within the declared specimen range. I’m now testing the last justified profile family—uniaxial microstrain along the three allowed principal axes—one phase at a time.

The only supported anisotropy is Mn₂WO₆ uniaxial microstrain along [100] (equatorial term at 8000 ppm, axial ≈1604 ppm), giving fresh Rwp 13.70%. MnWO₄ and WO₃ anisotropy collapsed to their isotropic bounds. I’m now checking the fresh residual for any coherent unassigned reflection family before declaring phase closure.

All remaining strong residuals map to the accepted Mn₂WO₆ reflections; there is no new coherent family. The mismatch is structural intensity (positive 19.88/20.42° and overpredicted 21.37°), so I’m making the final allowed structure test: oxygen shifts capped at 0.003 and 0.005 fractional coordinates with explicit Mn–O/W–O restraints.

The tightly restrained oxygen starts do not survive replay as improvements (best 13.71% versus 13.70%) and introduce W–O distances down to 1.62 Å, so both are rejected. Before finalizing, I’m doing one last closure check by adding the exact Mn-oxide precursor/intermediate references to the accepted three-phase state; only a stable nonzero scale would justify a fourth phase.

The closure check finds a real weak MnO family rather than scale collapse: its 111/220/311/222 lines coincide with residuals near 35.11, 59.32, 70.09, and 74.29°, and fresh replay improves to 13.62% at ~1.35 wt%. Mn₂O₃ and Mn₃O₄ are poorer. I’m stabilizing the MnO size/scale with a fixed physical size grid before deciding its reported fraction.

The MnO size grid is stable: 0.08–0.20 µm gives essentially the same replayed fit and 1.1–1.4 wt% MnO, with 0.08 µm marginally best (Rwp 13.617%). This supports a weak fourth phase, but its fraction is profile-sensitive; I’ll report that uncertainty. I’m checking the refined MnO cell against the allowed reference mismatch before freezing the final model.

The free MnO cell moved ~1.0% from its registered 4.410 Å value, outside the laboratory’s stated ±0.5% reference-mismatch envelope. I’m therefore taking the physical boundary model (a = 4.43205 Å): fresh Rwp 13.657%, fractions 61.21% MnWO₄, 20.99% WO₃, 16.85% Mn₂WO₆, and 0.95% MnO. The lower fit of the unconstrained cell is not being used.

Before sealing the report, I’m auditing one remaining topology-coverage risk: whether the frozen index contains any A₃WO₆ parent in the same P2₁2₁2₁ topology that could preserve the excellent peak positions while closing the 1:1 Mn:W balance better than Mn₂WO₆. This is a targeted frozen-index check, not a renewed broad search.

The requested artifacts are now assembled from the independent final replay. I’m running the last consistency audit: CIF parsing/geometry, fraction normalization, full-grid fit identity, project hash stability, and exact agreement between submitted CIFs and the four phases in `final.gpx`.

The audit found one issue worth correcting: the submitted WO₃ passes occupancy/contact hard checks but triggers an oxide O–O close-contact review at 1.585 Å. I’m tracing whether that contact came from the bounded coordinate refinement or the registered parent; I will roll WO₃ back to the last geometry-clean checkpoint if needed, even at a small Rwp cost.

I’m completing the final physical audit. One WO₃ reference has an implausibly short O–O contact, so I’m comparing geometry-clean monoclinic WO₃ parents in the same four-phase refinement before locking the submission.

The clean WO₃ records form the same monoclinic framework in alternate cells, while the current record is structurally non-equivalent despite similar peak positions. I’m now testing both clean variants against the unchanged MnWO₄/Mn₂WO₆/MnO state, with equivalent profile treatment, so the decision rests on both diffraction fit and geometry.

The ambient neutron WO₃ parent (COD 2311041) is geometry-clean and already improves the fit (Rwp ≈13.57% versus 13.66%) with a modest [10−1] texture term. The high-pressure variant imported malformed ionic type labels as hydrogen and its first refinement failed; I’m repairing that import rather than treating a numerical failure as evidence against the topology.

The clean ambient structure remains favored. A tightly bounded W-only refinement improves Rwp to about 13.49% while preserving the source oxygen framework; allowing oxygen coordinates makes the fit worse and drives nearly every oxygen variable to its bounds, so that light-atom branch is rejected. I’m checking the W-only checkpoint’s bond geometry and replay stability now.

The selected clean WO₃ model passes the geometry screen, and the full-grid replay gives Rwp 13.505% with no profile flags. Its bounded W shifts are about 0.03 Å; oxygen coordinates stay at the neutron-reference values. I’m now removing the disabled superseded WO₃ record from the project and doing a final scale/background-only convergence check.

The cleaned four-phase project now replays exactly on all 8,301 measured points: Rwp 13.505%, Rp 8.349%, with crystalline fractions 61.62% MnWO₄, 20.81% WO₃, 16.69% Mn₂WO₆, and 0.88% MnO. I’ve regenerated all submitted CIFs and profiles from that exact project; the remaining step is cross-file and physical-validation checks.

The content checks passed: all four CIFs parse, all pass the contact screen, fractions sum exactly to one, and `fit.csv` matches the original grid. One diagnostic GSAS-II accessor rewrote the project pickle while inspecting flags, so I’m restoring the verified checkpoint and rerunning the immutable replay/hash check before handoff.

